# /etc/argus-core/argus.env - the Argus core container's environment on the appliance.
#
# On a deployed VM this file is WRITTEN BY THE FIRST-BOOT SETUP - you don't create it by hand. Kept
# here as a reference for the contract. Root-only (0600): it carries the Zabbix API token and the
# at-rest encryption key. Docker reads it via --env-file (argus-core.service); systemd deliberately
# does NOT parse it (passwords may contain characters systemd's EnvironmentFile would mangle).
#
# What the setup writes:
#ARGUS_ZABBIX_API_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080/api_jsonrpc.php
#ARGUS_ZABBIX_API_TOKEN=<generated: the argus-svc super-admin API token>
#ARGUS_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
#ARGUS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<used once to seed the first admin, then scrubbed from this file>
#ARGUS_SECRET_KEY=<generated: at-rest encryption key - keep stable>
#ARGUS_UPDATE_DIR=/update
#ARGUS_CA_CERT_FILE=/ca/ca.crt
#ARGUS_CA_KEY_FILE=/ca/ca.key
#
# host.docker.internal resolves to the Docker bridge gateway (see argus-core.service --add-host), i.e.
# the VM itself - where nginx serves the Zabbix frontend/API on :8080. Public URL and timezone are NOT
# env-locked on the appliance: the setup seeds them through the Argus API so they stay editable in
# Settings. Add more ARGUS_* variables here (see argus/README.md for the full table) and
# `systemctl restart argus-core` to apply - e.g. ARGUS_COOKIE_SECURE/ARGUS_TRUST_PROXY/ARGUS_RP_* once
# you put an HTTPS reverse proxy in front.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /etc/argus-core/image.env - the OTHER env file (systemd-parsed, simple KEY=VALUE only): image/tag
# pins for the two container units. Uncomment to pin instead of tracking latest; apply with
# `systemctl restart argus-core argus-updater`.
#ARGUS_CORE_TAG=latest
#ARGUS_CORE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/g-guglielmi/argus
#ARGUS_UPDATER_TAG=latest
#ARGUS_UPDATER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/g-guglielmi/argus-updater
